<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>You are going to show <m>7x + 3y \le 21</m> on a grid, shading the unwanted region.</p><p>(a) The boundary line crosses the <m>x</m>-axis at <m>x =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="ans1"/>  </p><p>(b) It crosses the <m>y</m>-axis at <m>y =</m> <fillin characters="5" name="ans2"/>  </p><p>(c) How should you draw the line, and which side should you shade?</p><div><ol marker="(1)">
<li>Draw a dashed line and shade the side that does not contain <m>(0, 0)</m>.</li>
<li>Draw a dashed line and shade the side that contains <m>(0, 0)</m>.</li>
<li>Draw a solid line and shade the side that does not contain <m>(0, 0)</m>.</li>
<li>Draw a solid line and shade the side that contains <m>(0, 0)</m>.</li>
</ol>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>The boundary is <m>7x + 3y = 21</m>. (a) Put <m>y = 0</m>: <m>{7}x = {21}</m>, so <m>x = {3}</m>. (b) Put <m>x = 0</m>: <m>{3}y = {21}</m>, so <m>y = {7}</m>.</p><p>(c) The sign is <m>\le</m>, so points on the line are included: draw it solid. Test <m>(0, 0)</m>: <m>0 {\le} {21}</m> is true, so <m>(0, 0)</m> is in the required region <m>R</m>, which we leave clear. Shade the side that does not contain <m>(0, 0)</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>3</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>7</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>(3)</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>